        "raw_text": "\n--- Page 188 ---\nIO2 c.] POTENTIAL BETWEEN TWO NEARLY FLAT SURFACES. 153\nwhere $\\lambda$ is to be determined so that at every point of the field\n$$\\frac{d f}{d x}+\\frac{d g}{d y}+\\frac{d h}{d z}=0$$\n$(26)$\nand also so that the line-integral\n$$4 \\pi \\int\\left(f \\frac{d x}{d s}+g \\frac{d y}{d s}+h \\frac{d z}{d s}\\right) d s$$\n$(27)$\ntaken along any line of induction from the surface $a$ to the\nsurface $b$, shall be equal to -1 .\nLet us assume\n$$\\lambda=1+A+B(z-a)+C^{\\prime}(z-a)^{2}$$\n(28)\nand let us neglect powers and products of $A, B, C$, and at this\nstage of our work powers and products of the first derivatives of\n$a$ and $b$.\nThe solenoidal condition then gives\n$$B=-\\nabla^{2} a, \\quad C=-\\frac{1}{2} \\frac{\\nabla^{2}(b-a)}{b-a}$$\n(29)\nwhere\n$$\\nabla^{2}=-\\left(\\frac{d^{2}}{d x^{2}}+\\frac{d^{2}}{d y^{2}}\\right)$$\n(30)\nIf instead of taking the line-integral along the new line of\ninduction, we take it along the old line of induction, parallel to\nz, the second condition gives\nHence\n$$\\begin{gathered}\n1=1+A+\\frac{1}{2} B(b-a)+\\frac{1}{3} C(b-a)^{2} \\\\\nA=\\frac{1}{6}(b-a) \\nabla^{2}(2 a+b)\n\\end{gathered}$$\n(31)\nand\n$$\\lambda=1+\\frac{1}{8}(b-a) \\nabla^{2}(2 a+b)-(z-a) \\nabla^{2} a-\\frac{1}{2} \\frac{(z-a)^{2}}{b-a} \\nabla^{2}(b-a) .$$\n(32)\nWe thus find for the second approximation to the components\nof displacement,\n$$\\left.\\begin{array}{rl}\n-4 \\pi f & =\\frac{\\lambda}{b-a}\\left[\\frac{d a}{d x}+\\frac{d(b-a)}{d x} \\frac{z-a}{b-a}\\right] \\\\\n-4 \\pi g & =\\frac{\\lambda}{b-a}\\left[\\frac{d a}{d y}+\\frac{d(b-a)}{d y} \\frac{z-a}{b-a}\\right] \\\\\n4 \\pi h & =\\frac{\\lambda}{b-a}\n\\end{array}\\right\\}$$\n(33)\nand for the second approximation to the potential,\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\Psi=\\frac{z-a}{b-a}+\\frac{1}{8} \\nabla^{2}(2 a+b)(z-a)-\\frac{1}{2} & \\nabla^{2} a \\frac{(z-a)^{2}}{b-a} \\\\\n& -\\frac{1}{8} \\nabla^{2}(b-a) \\frac{(z-a)^{3}}{(b-a)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}$$\n(34)\n",
